WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:02.850
- [Narrator] If you're returning
to the office this summer,

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:06.180
you might be coming back
to a chilly welcome.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:08.490
Office temperatures have
long been a common gripe

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:11.010
among workers and might have an impact

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.660
on how productive they are.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.480
- I think you'd be shocked
to see how many people

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.550
take time out of their day

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.470
to try to adjust their environment

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.930
so that they can actually
focus on their work.

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.250
- [Narrator] This is Robert Bean,

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.070
he's a fellow and lecturer at ASHRAE.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:27.900
An organization that creates standards

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.900
for things like temperature
and ventilation in buildings.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:32.580
He's also an expert on their standard

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.350
for creating a workplace climate

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:36.510
which is typically used by offices.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:39.060
Bean will walk us through
how the factors they consider

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:42.090
need to balance out to get
the climate just right.

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:44.280
And by throwing off just one factor

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.630
can leave your office feeling

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:47.403
like it's still stuck in winter.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.380
- When we use air temperature
as the sole metric, you know,

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:53.910
what's on the thermostat on the wall

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:56.190
as our sole proxy for thermal comfort.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.530
It's like saying baking soda is a cake.

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:01.350
We're missing all of
the other ingredients.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:02.183
- [Narrator] To figure out

00:01:02.183 --> 00:01:03.900
how to make a space more comfortable,

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.790
ASHRAE looks at a number of factors

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:08.160
that can influence the thermal climate.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.890
First, environmental factors.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:15.210
Air speed measures how fast
the air moves in a space

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:17.250
while mean radiant temperature

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.110
looks at the average of the temperatures

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:22.230
of different surfaces and
things like air temperature

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:25.020
and humidity are also measured.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.723
- If you think about
your own day-to-day lives

00:01:27.723 --> 00:01:31.050
and your thermal experience,
and you feel drafts,

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:34.440
and you feel cold floors,
and you feel the heat coming

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.420
through the window, which
is radiant temperature

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.780
not air temperature, there's
many, many ingredients.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.580
- [Narrator] Environmental
factors are combined

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:45.150
with two personal factors,
which are a little more obvious.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:46.650
One is metabolic rate.

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:48.960
That's how fast your body processes energy

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.670
and you can be hotter or colder

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:52.650
depending on how much you move.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:54.510
The other is clothing insulation.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:55.950
If you're wearing light clothes,

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.800
you'll be colder than someone
wearing heavier clothes.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.440
- And that's the
challenge that we have for

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.090
indoor environmental
consultants is to really

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:05.070
understand these people,
what are they wearing?

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.420
What are they doing?

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.280
How long are they gonna be there?

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.460
And how does the environment around them

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.560
going to affect their sensation

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:15.480
and then ultimately their perception

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:16.620
- [Narrator] But to get the personal

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.230
and environmental factors into balance,

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:22.440
ASHRAE also considers
local discomfort factors

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.000
which help determine the specifics

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.950
of what make people uncomfortable.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:27.030
- We don't wanna have drafts.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:29.160
We don't wanna have cold floors, right?

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.010
We don't want it to be so dry or so humid.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:33.360
They'll tell you what you want.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.520
But more importantly, they'll
tell you what they don't want.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:37.380
- [Narrator] Bean says,
one source of discomfort

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:39.420
is when surfaces are
different temperatures

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.640
which is called radiant asymmetry.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:43.740
That's what happens if you're
sitting next to a window

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:45.930
and one arm gets warmer than the other.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:48.510
Another is draft, which
mostly affects the parts

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:51.030
of your body that aren't
covered by clothes.

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:52.560
Like that skin on your ankle,

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.440
where your pants don't
quite meet your shoe.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.590
When that ankle is colder
than say your head,

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.600
that's called vertical air temperature.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.910
Another factor ASHRAE tries to measure.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:07.200
- We wanna be able to create
a space using architecture,

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.850
enclosure design, interior design,

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:12.720
and mechanical systems to
create these boundary conditions

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.720
that are diverse, but they allow people

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:19.080
to experience the space without stress.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:20.910
That's the end-goal.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:23.070
- [Narrator] So, that's what
ASHRAE takes into account.

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:24.330
But if there's so much thought

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:26.100
put into your workplace climate,

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.220
then why can your office
still feel so cold?

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:31.410
- It's really like a domino effect.

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:35.340
So we know that in buildings,
for example, that have a lot

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:38.100
of glass, the mean rain
temperature is gonna go up

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.560
because the sunlight is
heating up all that glass.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.780
So, what people do is they
crank the air temperature down.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:45.360
So, they're trying to balance that out.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:46.560
- [Narrator] For many buildings,

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.300
overcompensating with the AC

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.640
might be the reason you feel too cold.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:53.550
A 2009 study of 95 Office Buildings

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:55.770
found that most set the thermostat colder

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:57.450
than guidelines suggest.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:59.220
There isn't an across the board reason,

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.110
the AC gets turned too low,

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:03.810
but researchers from Center
for the Built Environment

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:06.510
say that poorly designed
or maintained units

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:08.310
could be part of the problem.

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:11.070
But beyond the AC, changes in any factor

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:13.740
can make you feel colder,
not moving around,

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.210
sitting too close to event,

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:17.250
or wearing clothes that are too light.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:20.040
Some studies also suggest
that thermostat settings

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:22.200
may be skewed toward men's preferences

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.930
who tend to wear longer,
heavier layers in the office

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:27.810
and can have a naturally
faster metabolic rate.

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:31.110
- We know that it's impossible
to make everybody happy.

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:35.040
What the standard can do
when it's properly applied

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.530
is that we can create conditions

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:41.400
so that the majority of
people would be satisfied.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.650
- [Narrator] Some experts say
that turning the thermostat up

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:46.740
a few degrees and allowing
people to adapt their spaces

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:50.190
to suit their own comfort is
one solution to cold offices.

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:51.570
- As soon as you're able to adapt,

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:52.740
then you'll be able to see

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.623
that the temperature doesn't need to be

00:04:54.623 --> 00:04:58.020
at such a low value that
it can allow to climb up.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:00.300
- [Narrator] Things like
providing personal fans at desks

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.430
can allow buildings to
keep the office warmer

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:04.440
without sacrificing comfort.

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:07.260
An annual program in Japan
loosens up dress codes.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:08.970
So, workers can wear lighter clothes

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:10.620
into the office to help reduce

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:12.180
the need for air conditioning.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:14.760
But if changing your office
thermostat, isn't an option.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:17.220
You can try asking to
move to a different seat.

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:19.230
You might be more
comfortable next to a window

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:21.360
where the radiant temperature is warmer

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:24.930
or in a spot with lower
air flow, away from event.

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:27.900
And if all else fails, it's not a bad idea

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:30.423
to keep that extra sweater at your desk.

00:05:30.423 --> 00:05:33.006
(gentle music)

